  "asr_text": "At the breakfast table, beside the pillow in the nurse's office, and in work messages during breaks, people want to stay able to talk to someone. Making phone-free time longer may mean fewer arguments over the basket at night, but a student whose phone is taken away may stop opening up beside that pillow. He can follow the rule during class, but if the shift messages he gets during breaks stop, his workplace may lose trust in him. The father asking the school for help also doesn't want to go through the silence after taking the phone away again. They may disagree about how long phones can be used, but they share a wish to keep someone to talk to. What message do you want to answer right away? When does it arrive, and who is it from?",
